WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.160
California Governor Gavin Newsom is expected to sign a law that would ban the sale of

00:06.160 --> 00:10.660
new gas powered cars in that state by the year 2035.

00:10.660 --> 00:14.640
It would not outlaw however those cars owned at the time.

00:14.640 --> 00:19.940
This comes on the heels of major federal legislation aimed at boosting the electric vehicle market

00:19.940 --> 00:23.520
and Illinois legislation attempting to do some of the same.

00:23.520 --> 00:27.200
But are consumers ready to make the switch?

00:27.200 --> 00:31.900
Joining us are Dave Sloan, President of the Chicago Automobile Trade Association and

00:31.900 --> 00:36.080
Jen Walling, Executive Director for the Illinois Environmental Council.

00:36.080 --> 00:38.240
We welcome both of you to Chicago tonight.

00:38.240 --> 00:40.760
So Jen, we've got the Inflation Reduction Act.

00:40.760 --> 00:45.600
We have recently passed Illinois legislation that had tax credits for electric vehicles.

00:45.600 --> 00:51.080
Just remind us of all the potential tax incentives here for those who want to purchase an electrical

00:51.080 --> 00:53.000
vehicle or a charging station.

00:53.600 --> 00:54.600
Absolutely.

00:54.600 --> 01:01.360
So the Climate and Agricultural Jobs Act that was passed last year has a $4,000 rebate for

01:01.360 --> 01:04.320
each vehicle that hasn't gotten a rebate before.

01:04.320 --> 01:06.960
Those are underway right now as of July 1st.

01:06.960 --> 01:12.000
There also is an up to 80% rebate for the installation of electric vehicle infrastructure

01:12.000 --> 01:14.240
in our homes.

01:14.240 --> 01:15.240
Those are easily Googleable.

01:15.240 --> 01:20.200
You can find them on Google, find the information out there, get it through the dealer.

01:20.200 --> 01:26.640
The Inflation Reduction Act added to that with an additional tax credit, $7,500 for

01:26.640 --> 01:33.000
a new vehicle and $4,000 for a used vehicle, as well as copious money for charging infrastructure

01:33.000 --> 01:35.680
throughout the country.

01:35.680 --> 01:40.640
And Illinois also passed tax credits for electric vehicle manufacturing to be located

01:40.640 --> 01:41.720
here in the state.

01:41.720 --> 01:44.720
So a number of incentives that can stack on top of each other.

01:44.720 --> 01:48.280
A lot of potential incentives, although Dave Sloan, let's take a look at the average price

01:48.280 --> 01:50.120
for an electric vehicle right now.

01:50.120 --> 01:54.480
I believe it's around $65,000, just over $65,000.

01:54.480 --> 02:00.520
And there's some worries that EV manufacturers might boost the price to offset this new federal

02:00.520 --> 02:01.840
tax credit.

02:01.840 --> 02:06.040
Do you think all these incentives will bring those prices down for consumers and make them

02:06.040 --> 02:07.040
more affordable?

02:07.040 --> 02:12.120
Well, one problem with these recent incentives is they're very restrictive as to where the

02:12.120 --> 02:19.280
batteries are, the materials for the batteries are extracted and also where the vehicles

02:19.280 --> 02:24.080
are made and then what's the income of the consumer.

02:24.080 --> 02:29.640
So we're hoping that over time those will become less restrictive and more available

02:29.640 --> 02:30.940
to more consumers.

02:30.940 --> 02:37.560
But I don't think you'll see the manufacturers raising the price to offset the incentive,

02:37.560 --> 02:43.920
but obviously they have been raising prices because of the cost of the materials.

02:43.920 --> 02:44.920
Right.

02:44.920 --> 02:47.560
A big issue with inflation and supply chain issues right now.

02:47.560 --> 02:54.320
So Jennifer, to that California law that is expected to be signed by the governor, 2035

02:54.320 --> 02:58.640
banning the sale of new gas-powered cars, how big of an impact could that have?

02:58.640 --> 03:00.880
I think California passing that is huge.

03:00.880 --> 03:05.200
California was first with the clean car standards that have been really important to encourage

03:05.200 --> 03:07.440
EV manufacturing in the state.

03:07.560 --> 03:11.080
I think that impact can't be underestimated.

03:11.080 --> 03:13.400
And I know Illinois is going to be far behind.

03:13.400 --> 03:21.760
We haven't even enacted the clean car standards yet or some of the heavy-duty truck standards.

03:21.760 --> 03:25.920
So I think we have a lot of work that we need to do in the state, but it is really promising

03:25.920 --> 03:29.200
and it'll increase the electric vehicle market here in Illinois.

03:29.200 --> 03:35.360
Dave Sloan, could an action like that in California move the market in a big way to EVs?

03:35.360 --> 03:41.880
Well, the point, what's more important is the auto manufacturers are all in on EVs.

03:41.880 --> 03:49.240
I mean, they are spending tens of billions of dollars investing in the EV technology,

03:49.240 --> 03:53.840
new EV vehicles, and they're coming fast here over the next few years.

03:53.840 --> 03:59.360
So I think, and we've seen, we produced the Chicago Auto Show, we've seen more and more

03:59.360 --> 04:05.520
people interested in EVs taking test drives and so on and indicating their interest.

04:05.520 --> 04:09.000
So I think the demand is going to be there.

04:09.000 --> 04:13.800
We'd always rather see consumer demand move the market rather than government mandate

04:13.800 --> 04:17.960
because EVs might not be good for everyone.

04:17.960 --> 04:24.640
You know, you can see someone in rural areas who have to tow things.

04:25.200 --> 04:32.320
EVs just might not be the right answer, but certainly for suburban and city applications,

04:32.320 --> 04:34.920
they would work much better.

04:34.920 --> 04:42.200
So it's hard in a state like California or even one like Illinois to have a blanket mandate,

04:42.200 --> 04:44.000
much rather see consumer demand.

04:44.000 --> 04:47.520
And it certainly seems like we're going that way.

04:47.520 --> 04:49.680
Jen Walling, I want to pick up on that point.

04:49.680 --> 04:54.320
I have a friend that just moved from LA to Chicago and he had to ship his electric vehicle

04:54.400 --> 04:57.120
here because he found there was no way to drive across the country.

04:57.120 --> 05:03.040
So far behind are we as a nation and Illinois as a state in setting up the infrastructure

05:03.040 --> 05:07.000
to be able to drive EVs wherever you want.

05:07.000 --> 05:11.280
Looking at the infrastructure in Illinois, there is infrastructure that accommodates

05:11.280 --> 05:12.960
most mileage.

05:12.960 --> 05:16.960
Some of it you are going to have to stop at a fast charging station for 20 minutes.

05:16.960 --> 05:20.680
You may have to stop for longer at some of the slower charging stations, but there's

05:20.680 --> 05:22.840
infrastructure that exists.

05:22.840 --> 05:24.160
We do need to bulk it up.

05:24.160 --> 05:26.640
We need to have more fast charging stations.

05:26.640 --> 05:30.320
We need to provide more routes and more information for people.

05:30.320 --> 05:36.040
I think that some of those consolidated maps are yet to be fully complete.

05:36.040 --> 05:39.240
And there's so much more charging that's coming.

05:39.240 --> 05:42.520
Some of the groups that are coming in that might be companies are not as transparent

05:42.520 --> 05:46.200
or open about where they're going to put their charging stations.

05:46.200 --> 05:50.960
And we wish we could see a little more of that so that we can plan as a state for when

05:50.960 --> 05:54.920
and where electric vehicles are going to go and where the infrastructure routes will

05:54.920 --> 05:55.920
go.

05:55.920 --> 06:00.880
But our state is making a lot of progress and people can confidently make a road trip with

06:00.880 --> 06:02.800
most vehicles in Illinois.

06:02.800 --> 06:03.800
Dave Sloan, what about that?

06:03.800 --> 06:10.240
How far away is the U.S. from being able to have an EV driver go from Yuma, Arizona to

06:10.240 --> 06:14.880
Laramie and not have to worry about finding a charging station?

06:14.880 --> 06:19.080
Well, you know, certainly more needs to be done.

06:19.600 --> 06:21.840
The cars are smart, too.

06:21.840 --> 06:23.600
There's so much intelligence in the vehicles.

06:23.600 --> 06:27.240
They'll be able to tell you how much charge you have left and where you need to go to

06:27.240 --> 06:29.160
get your car fast charged.

06:29.160 --> 06:30.720
We'll start seeing things like that.

06:30.720 --> 06:36.400
So lots to be done, though, in terms of infrastructure.

06:36.400 --> 06:40.720
If you're a condo owner and is your condo association going to let you put a level two

06:40.720 --> 06:48.520
charger in your garage, those kinds of things need to happen for more people to get into

06:48.600 --> 06:50.800
the EV market.

06:50.800 --> 06:53.520
How easy is it for apartment dwellers?

06:53.520 --> 06:59.400
There's a company called Escalant that does a lot of research in the EV category.

06:59.400 --> 07:02.880
And they say that most new car buyers are homeowners.

07:02.880 --> 07:11.280
So that will, you know, so you're assuming they have garage to charge their vehicles.

07:11.280 --> 07:18.000
I've been driving an electric vehicle since 2013 and I charge my vehicle at my garage.

07:18.000 --> 07:22.120
I charge it at work and it works out well for me.

07:22.120 --> 07:26.120
Jen, while you mentioned that Illinois is behind the curve on just enacting mileage

07:26.120 --> 07:30.440
tangent, things like that, are groups like yours going to lobby the General Assembly

07:30.440 --> 07:35.920
for a law like the one being considered in California banning the sale of new gas powered

07:35.920 --> 07:37.880
cars down the road?

07:37.880 --> 07:41.560
The dominant will be lobbying on transportation issues.

07:41.560 --> 07:44.960
But we are far behind California, as I mentioned.

07:45.040 --> 07:47.280
I have to do my due diligence is starting.

07:47.280 --> 07:52.640
We need to make active transit and bike safety and public transit better first.

07:52.640 --> 07:58.000
Those need to be priorities, but electrification is vital to the transportation sector.

07:58.000 --> 07:59.640
We're going to need it.

07:59.640 --> 08:03.440
And there's so much in terms of the car standards, the alternative clean truck

08:03.440 --> 08:06.320
standards for one of several states.

08:06.320 --> 08:11.440
There's a bipartisan group of governors that signed on to a heavy duty MOU for semi trucks

08:11.440 --> 08:12.440
and light trucks.

08:12.440 --> 08:16.800
That's really important for environmental justice communities, particularly in Chicago.

08:16.800 --> 08:20.600
So those are all things that we're going to need to do within our state.

08:20.600 --> 08:26.280
The governor Pritzker and his team have really led on new policies like manufacturing.

08:26.280 --> 08:31.760
But there are a number of other policies that we need to put into place before we're heading

08:31.760 --> 08:33.000
in that direction.

08:33.000 --> 08:37.760
So I'm hoping we see the General Assembly and the IEPA take action on those.

08:37.760 --> 08:38.760
All right.

08:38.760 --> 08:39.760
Very interesting discussion.

08:39.760 --> 08:40.760
We're going to have to leave it there.

08:40.760 --> 08:42.960
Dave Sloan and Jen Walling, we appreciate it.
